%N A400204 Minimum perimeter of a right-angled triangle with integer sides whose area is divisible by the n-th prime.
%C A400204 This sequence records the minimum perimeter (a + b + c) among all Pythagorean triangles with integer sides satisfying a^2 + b^2 = c^2, whose area (a*b)/2 is a multiple of prime(n).
%C A400204 By parameterizing the sides using Euclid's formula where a = d*(m^2-k^2), b = d*(2mk), and c = d*(m^2+k^2), the divisibility constraint requires prime(n) to divide the area expression d^2*m*k*(m-k)*(m+k).
%C A400204 For all primes p >= 11 (n >= 5), the linear perimeter growth obtained by scaling the primitive 3-4-5 triangle by a factor of p (yielding 12*p) is strictly less than the quadratic perimeter lower bound of any custom primitive triangle satisfying p | m*k*(m-k)*(m+k), which is bounded below by p^2 + p. Hence, for n >= 5, a(n) is identically 12 * prime(n).
%F A400204 a(n) = 12 for n = 1, 2.
%F A400204 a(3) = 30.
%F A400204 a(4) = 56.
%F A400204 a(n) = 12 * A000040(n) for n >= 5.
%e A400204 For n = 1 (prime = 2): The standard primitive 3-4-5 right triangle has an area of (3*4)/2 = 6. Since 6 is divisible by 2, this triangle satisfies the constraint. Its perimeter is 3 + 4 + 5 = 12, which is the absolute minimum possible. Thus, a(1) = 12.
%e A400204 For n = 3 (prime = 5): The area of the 3-4-5 triangle (6) is not a multiple of 5. The primitive 5-12-13 right triangle has an area of (5*12)/2 = 30, which is divisible by 5. Its perimeter is 5 + 12 + 13 = 30. This is smaller than scaling the 3-4-5 triangle by 5 (which would give a perimeter of 12 * 5 = 60). Thus, a(3) = 30.
%t A400204 a[n_] := With[{p = Prime[n]}, If[n == 1 || n == 2, 12, If[n == 3, 30, If[n == 4, 56, 12*p]]]]
%o A400204 (Python)
%o A400204 from sympy import prime
%o A400204 def a(n):
%o A400204     if n == 1 or n == 2: return 12
%o A400204     if n == 3: return 30
%o A400204     if n == 4: return 56
%o A400204     return 12 * prime(n)
%o A400204 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 36)])
%Y A400204 Cf. A000040.
%K A400204 nonn,easy,new
